{
  "id": "COMPOUND:27962 - PROTEIN:5832",
  "verbalization": "n-Octyl sulfate appears to interact with CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, a protein that is targeted by multiple compounds, including (1R,8S,9R,16R)-8,16-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)-9-[(1R,8S,9R,16R)-4,6,12-trihydroxy-8,16-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)-15-oxatetracyclo[8.6.1.02,7.014,17]heptadeca-2(7),3,5,10(17),11,13-hexaen-9-yl]-15-oxatetracyclo[8.6.1.02,7.014,17]heptadeca-2(7),3,5,10(17),11,13-hexaene-4,6,12-triol and 2-Methylheptyl propanoate. These compounds are all implicated in the regulation of sialylation, a process that involves the addition of sialic acid groups to glycoproteins. The sialylation pathway is known to be involved in various cellular functions, including cell surface recognition and signaling. The fact that multiple compounds targeting this enzyme lead to the same final target, HuT78, suggests a potential mechanism by which n-Octyl sulfate may influence cellular processes through this pathway. The evidence supports a role for n-Octyl sulfate in modulating sialylation, potentially through its interaction with the enzyme CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, which in turn may affect the function of HuT78.",
